/* Microbiology 11–13: Other bacteria, zoonoses, other viruses, infection control, AMR */
Z("micro11",0,"m",["Rickettsiae are tiny obligate intracellular bacteria spread by arthropods (lice, fleas, ticks, mites) causing typhus and spotted fevers; scrub typhus (Orientia tsutsugamushi) is the commonest in India and leaves a black eschar.","They live inside blood vessel lining cells, damaging them and causing fever, rash and leaky vessels; the bite of a chigger mite leaves a black cigarette-burn-like scar (eschar) in scrub typhus.",["Typhus group: epidemic (R. prowazekii — louse; Brill–Zinsser recurrence), endemic/murine (R. typhi — flea).","Spotted fever group: Indian tick typhus (R. conorii), RMSF (R. rickettsii) — ticks.","Scrub typhus: Orientia tsutsugamushi, larval trombiculid mite (chigger); fever, eschar, lymphadenopathy, pneumonitis, myocarditis, meningoencephalitis; common cause of AES and PUO in India.","Lab: Weil–Felix (OXK for scrub), IgM ELISA (scrub typhus — test of choice), IFA (gold standard), PCR (eschar); treatment doxycycline (azithromycin in pregnancy)."],"Scrub typhus = 'Chigger mite, black eschar, OXK, doxycycline'."],
"Rickettsiae are small Gram-negative, obligate intracellular coccobacilli that multiply in the cytoplasm (and nucleus in spotted fever group) of endothelial cells, transmitted by arthropod vectors. Classification: typhus group — Rickettsia prowazekii (epidemic typhus; body louse Pediculus humanus corporis; infected louse faeces rubbed into bite; recrudescence years later as Brill–Zinsser disease; flying squirrels in USA), R. typhi (endemic/murine typhus; rat flea Xenopsylla cheopis); spotted fever group — R. rickettsii (Rocky Mountain spotted fever; ticks), R. conorii (Indian tick typhus/Mediterranean spotted fever; ticks; tache noire eschar), R. akari (rickettsialpox; mites); scrub typhus group — Orientia tsutsugamushi (larval trombiculid mites — chiggers of Leptotrombidium; reservoir rodents; 'scrub typhus triangle'). Coxiella and Bartonella were formerly included. Pathogenesis: invasion and multiplication in vascular endothelium → vasculitis, increased permeability, oedema, hypovolaemia, microthrombi, DIC; rash from vascular damage. Clinical: fever, headache, myalgia, rash (typhus — starts on trunk and spreads centrifugally sparing palms and soles; spotted fever — starts on extremities including palms and soles and spreads centripetally); scrub typhus — painless eschar at bite site (axilla, groin, genitals), regional lymphadenopathy, hepatosplenomegaly, maculopapular rash, complications — pneumonitis/ARDS, myocarditis, acute kidney injury, meningoencephalitis (a leading cause of acute encephalitis syndrome in parts of India, e.g., Gorakhpur), septic shock; fatality up to 30% untreated. Laboratory diagnosis: Weil–Felix reaction (cheap but insensitive; OXK positive in scrub typhus, OX19 in typhus); IgM ELISA for scrub typhus (test of choice in India), indirect immunofluorescence assay (gold standard), immunoperoxidase; PCR on blood or eschar (56-kDa gene for Orientia); isolation in cell culture or embryonated egg yolk sac/mice (BSL-3); skin biopsy immunohistochemistry; supportive — thrombocytopenia, transaminitis. Treatment: doxycycline (drug of choice, all ages), azithromycin (pregnancy), chloramphenicol. Prevention: vector control, repellents, protective clothing, chemoprophylaxis.",
[["Classification",[["Group","Species","Disease","Vector"],["Typhus","R. prowazekii","Epidemic typhus, Brill–Zinsser","Body louse"],["Typhus","R. typhi","Endemic (murine) typhus","Rat flea"],["Spotted fever","R. conorii","Indian tick typhus","Tick"],["Spotted fever","R. rickettsii","RMSF","Tick"],["Spotted fever","R. akari","Rickettsialpox","Mite"],["Scrub typhus","Orientia tsutsugamushi","Scrub typhus","Chigger (larval mite)"]]],
["Laboratory diagnosis",["IgM ELISA (scrub typhus)","IFA — gold standard","PCR on blood/eschar","Weil–Felix (OXK, OX19, OX2)","Culture (BSL-3)"]]],
{c:"Rickettsial infections, especially scrub typhus, are important treatable causes of acute febrile illness in India; an eschar and IgM ELISA with early doxycycline save lives."});

Z("micro11",1,"m",["Leptospirosis is a zoonosis caused by Leptospira interrogans, a spirochaete shed in the urine of rats and other animals; humans get infected through cuts or mucosa exposed to contaminated water or soil, especially during floods.","Rat urine contaminates floodwater; the corkscrew bacteria swim through skin breaks and reach the blood, liver, kidneys and lungs.",["Thin, tightly coiled spirochaete with hooked ends ('question mark'); seen by dark-ground microscopy; grows on EMJH/Korthof medium.","Biphasic illness: leptospiraemic phase (fever, myalgia — calf pain, conjunctival suffusion) → immune phase (meningitis, uveitis).","Weil's disease: jaundice, renal failure, haemorrhage; pulmonary haemorrhage syndrome.","Lab: blood/CSF (first week) and urine (second week) dark-ground and culture; MAT (gold standard serology), IgM ELISA, PCR. Treatment: doxycycline, penicillin; doxycycline prophylaxis."],"Leptospirosis = 'Rat urine, calf pain, red eyes, Weil's: jaundice + kidney'."],
"Leptospira interrogans (pathogenic; over 250 serovars, e.g., icterohaemorrhagiae, canicola, pomona) are thin (0.1 µm), tightly coiled, highly motile spirochaetes with one or both ends hooked; seen by dark-ground or phase-contrast microscopy, silver impregnation or immunofluorescence; they grow slowly (up to 13 weeks) aerobically at 28–30 °C in semisolid media — Ellinghausen–McCullough–Johnson–Harris (EMJH), Korthof, Fletcher. Leptospirosis is a worldwide zoonosis; maintenance hosts (rats — most important, cattle, dogs, pigs) harbour leptospires in renal tubules and excrete them in urine. Humans are infected by contact of abraded skin, conjunctiva or mucosa with contaminated water, soil or animal tissues — farmers (paddy fields), sewer workers, veterinarians, abattoir workers; outbreaks after floods and heavy rains (Kerala, Gujarat, Maharashtra, Andaman). Pathogenesis: leptospires penetrate and spread haematogenously, cause vasculitis with capillary endothelial damage, multiply in liver, kidney, lungs, CNS, muscles. Clinical: incubation 7–12 days; anicteric leptospirosis (90%) — biphasic: leptospiraemic phase (abrupt fever, headache, severe myalgia especially calves, conjunctival suffusion, 4–7 days), immune phase (antibody appearance; aseptic meningitis, uveitis, rash); icteric leptospirosis (Weil's disease, 5–10%) — jaundice (with relatively mild enzyme rise), acute kidney injury, haemorrhage, myocarditis, severe pulmonary haemorrhage syndrome; mortality 5–15%. Laboratory diagnosis: first week — blood and CSF for dark-ground microscopy (low sensitivity) and culture; after first week — urine; serology — microscopic agglutination test (MAT, reference; live antigens of serovars; titre ≥ 1:400 or four-fold rise), IgM ELISA, rapid tests; PCR (early phase); haematology and biochemistry (neutrophilia, thrombocytopenia, raised creatinine, bilirubin, CPK). Treatment: doxycycline (mild), IV penicillin or ceftriaxone (severe), dialysis; prophylaxis with weekly doxycycline in high-risk exposure; rodent control, protective footwear.",
[["Phases",[["Phase","Timing","Specimen","Features"],["Leptospiraemic","Week 1","Blood, CSF","Fever, myalgia, conjunctival suffusion"],["Immune","Week 2+","Urine; serology","Meningitis, uveitis; Weil's disease"]]],
["Laboratory diagnosis",["Dark-ground microscopy","Culture: EMJH, Korthof, Fletcher (28–30 °C)","MAT (gold standard), IgM ELISA","PCR"]]],
{c:"Leptospirosis should be suspected in febrile patients after floodwater or animal exposure; IgM ELISA/MAT and early antibiotics reduce severe Weil's disease."});

Z("micro11",2,"m",["Borrelia are spirochaetes spread by lice or ticks: B. recurrentis causes louse-borne relapsing fever with repeated fever episodes, and B. burgdorferi causes Lyme disease with an expanding 'bull's-eye' rash.","Relapsing fever Borrelia keep changing their coats (antigenic variation), so each time the immune system clears them, a new variant brings back the fever.",["Larger, loosely coiled spirochaetes; stain with Giemsa/Wright (unlike Treponema).","Relapsing fever: B. recurrentis (body louse — epidemic), B. duttonii (soft tick — endemic); fever episodes with afebrile intervals; Jarisch–Herxheimer reaction on treatment.","Diagnosis of relapsing fever: Borrelia in thick/thin blood smears during fever.","Lyme disease: B. burgdorferi, Ixodes tick; erythema migrans, arthritis, carditis, facial palsy; diagnosis serology (ELISA + Western blot). Treatment: doxycycline, penicillin."],"Relapsing fever = 'Recurrent fever, antigenic variation, seen on blood smear'."],
"Borrelia are relatively large (10–30 × 0.2–0.5 µm), loosely coiled, irregular spirochaetes with 3–10 coils, motile by periplasmic flagella; unlike Treponema and Leptospira they stain readily with Giemsa, Wright or Leishman stains and are Gram-negative. Relapsing fever: louse-borne (epidemic) relapsing fever caused by B. recurrentis, transmitted by the body louse (infection by crushing lice and rubbing haemolymph into bite wounds; humans are only reservoir; occurs with war, famine, overcrowding; historically in India); tick-borne (endemic) relapsing fever caused by B. duttonii and other species, transmitted by soft ticks (Ornithodoros), with rodent reservoirs. Pathogenesis: spirochaetaemia with high fever; antibodies clear the organisms, but antigenic variation of variable major proteins produces new serotypes and relapses. Clinical: sudden high fever, headache, myalgia, hepatosplenomegaly, jaundice, petechiae, lasting 3–6 days; afebrile interval of about a week; 3–10 relapses of decreasing severity; complications — myocarditis, haemorrhage, DIC, meningitis. Treatment (tetracycline, penicillin, erythromycin) may provoke Jarisch–Herxheimer reaction. Diagnosis: demonstration of spirochaetes in thick and thin peripheral blood smears taken during febrile episodes (Giemsa, dark-ground), QBC, animal inoculation, PCR; serology unhelpful because of antigenic variation; B. recurrentis may give false-positive VDRL. Lyme disease: caused by Borrelia burgdorferi sensu lato, transmitted by hard ticks (Ixodes) from deer and rodent reservoirs (USA, Europe; rare in India); stages — early localised (erythema migrans: expanding annular 'bull's-eye' rash), early disseminated (multiple EM, facial nerve palsy, meningitis, carditis — AV block), late (arthritis of large joints, encephalopathy, acrodermatitis chronica atrophicans); diagnosis by two-tier serology (ELISA followed by Western blot), PCR of synovial fluid; treatment doxycycline, amoxicillin, ceftriaxone.",
[["Comparison",[["Feature","Relapsing fever","Lyme disease"],["Species","B. recurrentis, B. duttonii","B. burgdorferi"],["Vector","Body louse, soft tick","Hard tick (Ixodes)"],["Key feature","Recurrent fever (antigenic variation)","Erythema migrans, arthritis, carditis"],["Diagnosis","Blood smear during fever","Serology (ELISA + Western blot)"],["Treatment","Doxycycline, penicillin (J–H reaction)","Doxycycline, amoxicillin, ceftriaxone"]]]],
{c:"Relapsing fever is diagnosed by spirochaetes in blood smears during fever; Lyme disease by serology; both respond to doxycycline or penicillins."});

Z("micro11",3,"m",["Listeria monocytogenes is a Gram-positive rod found in food (soft cheese, deli meat) that grows even in the fridge; it causes serious infection in pregnant women, newborns, elderly and immunocompromised people.","A cold-tolerant bacterium that sneaks from refrigerated food into the blood, crossing the placenta and the blood–brain barrier by riding inside cells on 'actin rockets'.",["Gram-positive short bacilli; tumbling motility at 25 °C (umbrella motility in semisolid agar), non-motile at 37 °C; grows at 4 °C (cold enrichment).","Narrow β-haemolysis; catalase +, CAMP test positive (block-like); aesculin hydrolysis.","Virulence: internalin (entry), listeriolysin O (escape from phagosome), ActA (actin-based cell-to-cell spread).","Disease: febrile gastroenteritis, bacteraemia, meningitis (neonates, elderly, immunocompromised), pregnancy — amnionitis, abortion, granulomatosis infantiseptica. Treatment: ampicillin (cephalosporins ineffective)."],"Listeria = 'Loves the fridge, tumbling motility, ampicillin (not cephalosporins)'."],
"Listeria monocytogenes is a Gram-positive, non-sporing, short coccobacillus (often in palisades or V-shapes, may resemble diphtheroids), facultatively anaerobic and facultatively intracellular. It shows characteristic tumbling motility at 20–25 °C (peritrichous flagella) but is non-motile at 37 °C; in semisolid agar produces umbrella-shaped motility. It grows over a wide temperature range including 4 °C (cold enrichment used for isolation from contaminated specimens). On blood agar it produces small colonies with a narrow zone of β-haemolysis; catalase-positive (differentiating from streptococci), aesculin hydrolysis positive, CAMP test positive with S. aureus, Anton test (keratoconjunctivitis in rabbits). Habitat: soil, water, animals; transmitted by contaminated food — soft cheese, unpasteurised milk, deli meats, pâté, coleslaw, raw vegetables; also transplacental and during delivery. Virulence factors: internalins A and B (entry into epithelial cells and hepatocytes), listeriolysin O (pore-forming toxin enabling escape from phagosomes), phospholipases, ActA protein (polymerises host actin forming comet tails for cell-to-cell spread without exposure to antibodies), iron acquisition; cell-mediated immunity required for clearance. Diseases: febrile gastroenteritis in healthy people (outbreaks); invasive listeriosis in pregnant women (flu-like illness, bacteraemia, chorioamnionitis, abortion, stillbirth, preterm labour), neonates (early-onset — granulomatosis infantiseptica with disseminated abscesses and granulomas, sepsis; late-onset — meningitis), elderly and immunocompromised (transplant, steroids, malignancy, diabetes, HIV) — bacteraemia, meningitis, rhombencephalitis, brain abscess. Laboratory diagnosis: blood, CSF, placenta, meconium; Gram stain (may be misidentified as diphtheroid contaminant); culture on blood agar and selective media (PALCAM, Oxford agar, chromogenic), cold enrichment for food and contaminated samples; identification by motility, catalase, CAMP, MALDI-TOF; PCR. Treatment: ampicillin or penicillin plus gentamicin; co-trimoxazole in penicillin allergy; intrinsically resistant to cephalosporins (important in empirical meningitis therapy for neonates and elderly).",
[["Identification",["Gram-positive short bacilli","Tumbling motility at 25 °C; umbrella in semisolid agar","Narrow β-haemolysis; catalase +","CAMP +, aesculin +","Grows at 4 °C"]],
["High-risk groups",["Pregnant women — abortion, stillbirth","Neonates — granulomatosis infantiseptica, meningitis","Elderly, immunocompromised — meningitis, bacteraemia"]]],
{c:"Listeria is an intracellular food-borne pathogen dangerous to pregnancy, neonates and the immunocompromised; ampicillin must be added to empirical meningitis regimens in these groups."});

Z("micro11",4,"m",["Nocardia and Actinomyces are branching Gram-positive bacteria resembling fungi: Nocardia is aerobic, weakly acid-fast and causes lung and brain infections in the immunocompromised; Actinomyces is anaerobic, a mouth commensal, causing chronic abscesses with sulphur granules.","Both grow in branching threads like fungi; Actinomyces burrows through tissue planes forming sinuses with yellow 'sulphur' granules; Nocardia travels via blood to the brain.",["Nocardia: aerobic, soil saprophyte, partially (modified) acid-fast (1% H2SO4); pulmonary nocardiosis, brain abscess, actinomycetoma; co-trimoxazole.","Actinomyces israelii: anaerobic/microaerophilic, non-acid-fast, endogenous (oral flora).","Actinomycosis: cervicofacial ('lumpy jaw' after dental work), thoracic, abdominal, pelvic (IUCD); sinuses discharging sulphur granules.","Lab: Gram stain of granules (branching Gram-positive filaments), modified ZN (Nocardia +), anaerobic culture ('molar tooth' colonies — Actinomyces); treatment penicillin for Actinomyces."],"Nocardia = 'aerobic, acid-fast (weakly), co-trimoxazole'; Actinomyces = 'anaerobic, sulphur granules, penicillin'."],
"Nocardia and Actinomyces are Gram-positive bacteria forming branching filaments that fragment into bacillary and coccoid forms. Nocardia (N. asteroides complex, N. brasiliensis, N. farcinica) are strict aerobes, saprophytes of soil and decaying vegetation; they are partially (weakly) acid-fast — stained by modified Kinyoun/ZN using 1% sulphuric acid — and grow on ordinary media (blood agar, SDA without antibiotics, LJ) in 2–7 days with dry, chalky, wrinkled, orange or white colonies with earthy odour; also grow at 45 °C. Infection by inhalation (pulmonary nocardiosis — pneumonia, cavitation, empyema, mimicking TB; in immunocompromised — transplant, steroids, HIV, malignancy) with haematogenous spread to brain (brain abscess) and skin; traumatic inoculation causes cutaneous/subcutaneous infection and actinomycetoma (N. brasiliensis). Diagnosis: Gram and modified acid-fast stains of sputum, pus, biopsy; culture; MALDI-TOF, 16S rRNA sequencing. Treatment: co-trimoxazole (prolonged), imipenem, amikacin, linezolid. Actinomyces (A. israelii most common, A. gerencseriae, A. naeslundii) are anaerobic or microaerophilic, non-acid-fast, endogenous commensals of mouth, tonsils, GI tract and female genital tract. Actinomycosis is a chronic suppurative and granulomatous infection that spreads contiguously across tissue planes, ignoring anatomical barriers, forming abscesses, dense fibrosis and multiple draining sinuses discharging pus with yellow 'sulphur granules' (bacterial colonies with peripheral club-shaped Splendore–Hoeppli material). Forms: cervicofacial ('lumpy jaw', after dental extraction or poor oral hygiene; commonest), thoracic (aspiration), abdominal (after appendicitis or bowel surgery; ileocaecal), pelvic (associated with long-term IUCD use), CNS. Diagnosis: pus examined for sulphur granules, crushed and Gram-stained (Gram-positive branching filaments with clubbed ends); non-acid-fast; anaerobic culture on brain heart infusion or thioglycollate (white 'molar tooth' or 'breadcrumb' colonies after 7–14 days); histopathology; MALDI-TOF. Treatment: high-dose penicillin for prolonged periods (months), surgical drainage.",
[["Comparison",[["Feature","Nocardia","Actinomyces"],["Oxygen","Strict aerobe","Anaerobic/microaerophilic"],["Acid-fast (modified)","Positive (weak)","Negative"],["Habitat","Soil (exogenous)","Oral flora (endogenous)"],["Granules","Rare (in mycetoma)","Sulphur granules common"],["Spread","Haematogenous (brain)","Contiguous, sinuses"],["Host","Immunocompromised","Usually immunocompetent"],["Diseases","Pulmonary, brain abscess, mycetoma","Cervicofacial, thoracic, abdominal, pelvic"],["Treatment","Co-trimoxazole","Penicillin"]]]],
{c:"Branching Gram-positive filaments are distinguished by oxygen requirement and acid-fastness; correct identification directs very different antibiotic choices."});

Z("micro11",5,"m",["Non-sporing anaerobes such as Bacteroides fragilis are normal residents of the gut and mouth that cause pus-forming infections (abscesses) when they escape into tissues, especially along with other bacteria.","Bacteroides normally live peacefully in the oxygen-free gut; after a perforation or surgery they spill into tissues and form foul-smelling abscesses together with aerobic partners.",["Gram-negative anaerobic bacilli: Bacteroides fragilis (most important; capsule — abscess formation), Prevotella, Porphyromonas, Fusobacterium.","Gram-positive anaerobes: Peptostreptococcus, Actinomyces, Cutibacterium.","Infections: intra-abdominal abscess, peritonitis, liver abscess, aspiration pneumonia, lung abscess, brain abscess, PID, diabetic foot; Lemierre syndrome (Fusobacterium).","Clues: foul odour, gas in tissues, necrosis, mixed Gram stain, failure to grow aerobically; anaerobic culture (Robertson cooked meat, anaerobic jar/McIntosh–Fildes, GasPak); treatment metronidazole, β-lactam/β-lactamase inhibitors, carbapenems."],"Anaerobic infection clues: 'Foul smell, Gas, Necrosis, sterile aerobic culture'."],
"Non-sporing anaerobes are bacteria that grow only in the absence of oxygen and do not form spores; they predominate in the normal flora of the colon (Bacteroides outnumber E. coli 1000:1), mouth, upper respiratory tract, vagina and skin. Important groups: Gram-negative bacilli — Bacteroides fragilis group (gut; capsule polysaccharide promotes abscess formation; produces β-lactamase; resistant to bile; most clinically important), Prevotella (oral, genital), Porphyromonas (periodontal disease), Fusobacterium (oral; F. necrophorum — Lemierre syndrome — septic thrombophlebitis of internal jugular vein); Gram-positive cocci — Peptostreptococcus, Peptococcus; Gram-positive bacilli — Actinomyces, Cutibacterium (Propionibacterium) acnes, Eubacterium, Bifidobacterium; Gram-negative cocci — Veillonella. Infections are endogenous, usually polymicrobial (mixed with aerobes and facultative anaerobes, which consume oxygen and lower redox potential), arising when mucosal barriers are breached (surgery, trauma, perforation, malignancy) or blood supply is impaired. Infections: intra-abdominal (peritonitis, appendicular and subphrenic abscess, liver abscess after perforation), pelvic (PID, tubo-ovarian abscess, septic abortion, endometritis), pleuropulmonary (aspiration pneumonia, lung abscess, empyema), head and neck (dental abscess, Vincent angina, chronic sinusitis, otitis), brain abscess, diabetic and decubitus ulcers, necrotising soft tissue infections, bacteraemia. Clinical clues: foul-smelling discharge, gas in tissues, necrotic tissue/abscess, infection adjacent to mucosal surfaces, black discharge (Prevotella, Porphyromonas pigment), sulphur granules, failure of aminoglycosides, 'sterile' pus on aerobic culture with organisms on Gram stain. Laboratory diagnosis: specimens collected avoiding contamination with normal flora — pus aspirates and tissue (not swabs), transported in anaerobic transport media; Gram stain (pleomorphic Gram-negative bacilli); culture on enriched media (brain heart infusion blood agar, Bacteroides bile aesculin agar, kanamycin–vancomycin laked blood agar) in anaerobic conditions — McIntosh–Fildes jar, GasPak system, anaerobic chamber/workstation; Robertson cooked meat medium, thioglycollate broth; identification by special potency discs (vancomycin, kanamycin, colistin), biochemistry, gas–liquid chromatography, MALDI-TOF. Treatment: drainage and debridement; metronidazole, clindamycin, β-lactam/β-lactamase inhibitors (piperacillin–tazobactam), carbapenems.",
[["Important anaerobes",[["Group","Examples","Infections"],["Gram-negative bacilli","Bacteroides fragilis, Prevotella, Porphyromonas, Fusobacterium","Abdominal, pelvic, lung, brain abscesses; Lemierre"],["Gram-positive cocci","Peptostreptococcus","Mixed abscesses"],["Gram-positive bacilli","Actinomyces, Cutibacterium","Actinomycosis, device infections"]]],
["Anaerobic culture methods",["McIntosh–Fildes jar","GasPak (H2 + CO2 generator, palladium catalyst, methylene blue indicator)","Anaerobic chamber","Robertson cooked meat, thioglycollate broth"]]],
{c:"Anaerobic infections are usually endogenous and polymicrobial; proper specimen collection, anaerobic culture and drainage with metronidazole-based therapy are essential."});

Z("micro11",6,"m",["Zoonoses are infections naturally transmitted between vertebrate animals and humans; they include bacterial, viral, parasitic and fungal diseases, and are controlled through a One Health approach.","Animals act as reservoirs; humans become accidental guests through bites, milk, meat, vectors or contaminated water.",["Bacterial: anthrax, brucellosis, leptospirosis, plague, Q fever, bovine TB, salmonellosis, glanders, melioidosis.","Viral: rabies, Japanese encephalitis, KFD, Nipah, avian/swine influenza, Crimean–Congo HF, mpox.","Parasitic: hydatid disease, cysticercosis, toxoplasmosis, leishmaniasis (zoonotic forms), trichinosis; fungal: dermatophytes (M. canis), histoplasmosis.","Control: animal vaccination (rabies, anthrax, brucella), meat inspection, pasteurisation, vector and rodent control, PPE for workers, surveillance, One Health (IDSP)."],"Zoonoses India top: 'Rabies, Leptospirosis, JE, Brucella, Anthrax, Hydatid, KFD, Nipah'."],
"Zoonoses are diseases and infections naturally transmitted between vertebrate animals and humans (WHO). About 60% of human pathogens and 75% of emerging infections are zoonotic. Classification by agent: bacterial — anthrax (Bacillus anthracis), brucellosis, plague, leptospirosis, bovine tuberculosis, salmonellosis, campylobacteriosis, listeriosis, glanders, melioidosis, tularaemia, Q fever (Coxiella), cat-scratch disease (Bartonella), scrub typhus and other rickettsioses, psittacosis; viral — rabies, Japanese encephalitis, Kyasanur forest disease, West Nile, Crimean–Congo haemorrhagic fever, Nipah, avian and swine influenza, Ebola/Marburg, mpox, SARS-CoV, MERS-CoV, hantavirus; parasitic — toxoplasmosis, hydatidosis, taeniasis/cysticercosis, trichinellosis, zoonotic leishmaniasis, trypanosomiasis, fascioliasis; fungal — dermatophytosis (Microsporum canis), histoplasmosis, cryptococcosis, sporotrichosis. Classification by cycle: direct (orthozoonoses — rabies, brucellosis), cyclozoonoses (more than one vertebrate host — hydatid, taeniasis), metazoonoses (invertebrate vector — JE, plague, KFD), saprozoonoses (environment/inanimate reservoir — histoplasmosis); by direction: anthropozoonoses (animal to human), zooanthroponoses (human to animal), amphixenoses. Transmission: bites and scratches, direct contact with animals or tissues, consumption of contaminated milk, meat, eggs, inhalation, vectors, contaminated water and soil. High-risk groups: farmers, veterinarians, butchers, abattoir and dairy workers, laboratory personnel, forest workers. Control: surveillance (IDSP, National One Health Mission), control in animal reservoirs (vaccination — rabies, anthrax, brucellosis; test and slaughter; deworming dogs), control of vectors and rodents, food hygiene (meat inspection, pasteurisation, proper cooking), occupational protection (PPE), human vaccination (rabies PEP/PrEP, JE, KFD), health education, inter-sectoral coordination under One Health.",
[["Examples",[["Agent","Zoonoses","Animal source"],["Bacteria","Anthrax, brucellosis, leptospirosis, plague, Q fever, bovine TB","Cattle, goats, rats, fleas"],["Viruses","Rabies, JE, KFD, Nipah, avian influenza, mpox","Dogs, pigs/birds, monkeys/ticks, bats, poultry, rodents"],["Parasites","Hydatid, cysticercosis, toxoplasmosis, leishmaniasis","Dogs, pigs, cats"],["Fungi","Dermatophytosis, histoplasmosis","Cats/dogs, birds/bats"]]],
["Control measures",["Animal vaccination and control","Vector and rodent control","Meat inspection, pasteurisation","PPE for occupational groups","Surveillance and One Health coordination"]]],
{c:"Zoonoses dominate emerging infections; a One Health approach linking human, animal and environmental health is essential to their control."});

Z("micro11",7,"m",["Coxiella burnetii is an obligate intracellular bacterium that causes Q fever, usually acquired by inhaling dust contaminated by infected cattle, sheep or goats (especially birth products).","Coxiella forms tough spore-like particles that survive in dust for months; breathing them in causes a flu-like illness, pneumonia or chronic endocarditis.",["Not transmitted by arthropods to humans (unlike rickettsiae); inhalation of aerosols from placenta, milk, faeces.","Highly infectious (1 organism); resistant small-cell variant; phase variation (phase I antibodies — chronic, phase II — acute).","Acute Q fever: flu-like illness, atypical pneumonia, hepatitis; chronic: culture-negative endocarditis.","Lab: serology (IFA phase I and II antibodies), PCR; Weil–Felix negative; treatment doxycycline (+ hydroxychloroquine for endocarditis)."],"Q fever = 'Query fever: no rash, no vector, Weil–Felix negative, culture-negative endocarditis'."],
"Coxiella burnetii is a small, pleomorphic, Gram-negative, obligate intracellular bacterium that multiplies within phagolysosomes of macrophages and forms a highly resistant spore-like small-cell variant that survives for long periods in the environment and resists heat and disinfectants. It shows phase variation: phase I (virulent, natural infection, full LPS) and phase II (avirulent, after passage in eggs or cell culture). Reservoirs: cattle, sheep, goats (shed heavily in placenta, birth fluids, milk, urine, faeces), also ticks among animals. Human infection is mainly by inhalation of contaminated aerosols and dust (abattoir workers, farmers, veterinarians, laboratory workers), less often by raw milk; infectious dose very low (category B bioterrorism agent); no person-to-person spread and no rash. Clinical: acute Q fever (many asymptomatic) — flu-like illness with high fever, severe headache, myalgia; atypical pneumonia; granulomatous hepatitis ('doughnut' granulomas); chronic Q fever (months to years later, especially with valvular disease, vascular grafts, immunosuppression, pregnancy) — culture-negative infective endocarditis, vascular infections, osteomyelitis, chronic fatigue syndrome. Laboratory diagnosis: serology by indirect immunofluorescence (gold standard) — acute infection: phase II IgG/IgM antibodies higher; chronic infection: high phase I IgG titre (≥ 1:800); ELISA, CFT; PCR on blood, serum, tissue (heart valve); isolation in cell culture (BSL-3, not routine); Weil–Felix test negative. Treatment: doxycycline; chronic — doxycycline plus hydroxychloroquine for 18 months or more. Prevention: pasteurisation, protective measures, vaccine for high-risk workers (Australia).",
[["Features",["Obligate intracellular; spore-like resistant form","Reservoir: cattle, sheep, goats","Transmission: inhalation (no vector to humans)","No rash; Weil–Felix negative","Phase II antibodies → acute; phase I → chronic"]],
["Clinical forms",["Acute: flu-like, pneumonia, hepatitis","Chronic: culture-negative endocarditis, vascular infection"]]],
{c:"Q fever is an occupational zoonosis acquired by inhalation; phase-specific serology distinguishes acute from chronic infection and guides doxycycline-based therapy."});

Z("micro12",0,"m",["Measles, mumps and rubella are childhood viral infections (paramyxoviruses and a togavirus) prevented by the MMR/MR vaccine; measles causes fever with rash and severe complications, mumps causes parotitis, and rubella is mild but causes birth defects in pregnancy.","Measles is the dangerous one (pneumonia, encephalitis, death in malnourished children), mumps swells the salivary glands, rubella quietly harms the unborn baby.",["Measles (paramyxovirus): 3 Cs — cough, coryza, conjunctivitis; Koplik spots; maculopapular rash from face downward; complications — pneumonia, diarrhoea, encephalitis, SSPE; vitamin A.","Mumps (paramyxovirus): parotitis; orchitis (post-pubertal), meningitis, pancreatitis, deafness.","Rubella (togavirus): mild rash, post-auricular lymphadenopathy; congenital rubella syndrome — cataract, PDA, deafness.","Lab: IgM ELISA, RT-PCR; Warthin–Finkeldey giant cells (measles). India: MR vaccine at 9–12 and 16–24 months; measles–rubella elimination goal."],"Measles '3 Cs + Koplik'; Rubella 'Gregg triad: cataract, cardiac, cochlear'."],
"Measles virus (Morbillivirus, Paramyxoviridae) is an enveloped, non-segmented negative-sense RNA virus with H and F glycoproteins; single serotype; spread by respiratory droplets and aerosols; highly contagious (R0 12–18). Pathogenesis: replicates in respiratory epithelium and lymphoid tissue (SLAM/CD150 receptor), causes viraemia and multisystem infection; transient immunosuppression ('immune amnesia'). Clinical: incubation 10–14 days; prodrome of fever, cough, coryza, conjunctivitis; Koplik spots (bluish-white spots on red buccal mucosa — pathognomonic); maculopapular rash starting behind ears and face, spreading downward, fading with brown staining and desquamation; complications — pneumonia (giant cell pneumonia; secondary bacterial), otitis media, diarrhoea and malnutrition, laryngotracheobronchitis, keratitis and blindness (vitamin A deficiency), acute post-infectious encephalomyelitis, measles inclusion body encephalitis (immunocompromised), subacute sclerosing panencephalitis (SSPE; years later), death. Mumps virus (Rubulavirus, Paramyxoviridae; HN and F proteins) spreads by droplets and saliva; causes painful bilateral or unilateral parotitis, and orchitis/oophoritis (post-pubertal; rarely sterility), aseptic meningitis, encephalitis, pancreatitis, sensorineural deafness. Rubella virus (Rubivirus, Matonaviridae/formerly Togaviridae; positive-sense RNA) causes German measles: mild fever, maculopapular rash, post-auricular and suboccipital lymphadenopathy, arthralgia in adult women. Congenital rubella syndrome (maternal infection in first trimester): classic Gregg triad — cataract, cardiac defects (PDA, pulmonary artery stenosis), sensorineural deafness; plus microcephaly, intellectual disability, hepatosplenomegaly, thrombocytopenic purpura (blueberry muffin), 'salt and pepper' retinopathy; infants shed virus for months. Laboratory diagnosis: measles — IgM ELISA (from rash onset), RT-PCR on throat/nasopharyngeal swab or urine, multinucleated Warthin–Finkeldey giant cells; mumps — IgM ELISA, RT-PCR on saliva/buccal swab, raised amylase; rubella — IgM ELISA, IgG avidity, RT-PCR; CRS — infant IgM, persistent IgG, RT-PCR. Prevention: live attenuated MMR vaccine (MR in Indian UIP at 9–12 and 16–24 months; MR campaigns for elimination), vitamin A for measles cases, post-exposure vaccination within 72 h or immunoglobulin.",
[["Comparison",[["Feature","Measles","Mumps","Rubella"],["Family","Paramyxoviridae","Paramyxoviridae","Matonaviridae (Togavirus)"],["Hallmark","Koplik spots, 3 Cs, rash","Parotitis","Rash, post-auricular nodes"],["Complications","Pneumonia, encephalitis, SSPE","Orchitis, meningitis, deafness","Congenital rubella syndrome"],["Diagnosis","IgM, RT-PCR","IgM, RT-PCR","IgM, avidity, RT-PCR"],["Vaccine","MR/MMR","MMR","MR/MMR"]]]],
{c:"Measles, mumps and rubella are vaccine-preventable; high two-dose MR coverage and surveillance with IgM testing are the basis of India's elimination strategy."});

Z("micro12",1,"m",["Rotavirus and norovirus are the main viral causes of gastroenteritis: rotavirus causes severe watery diarrhoea in infants, norovirus causes outbreaks of vomiting and diarrhoea in all ages.","Rotavirus is the 'baby diarrhoea virus' — prevented by vaccine; norovirus is the 'cruise ship virus' — spreads explosively in closed groups.",["Rotavirus: reovirus, double-shelled 'wheel', 11 segments dsRNA; infants 6–24 months; NSP4 enterotoxin; watery diarrhoea, vomiting, dehydration.","Rotavirus diagnosis: stool antigen ELISA/ICT, EM, RT-PCR; vaccine (Rotavac, Rotasiil) in UIP at 6, 10, 14 weeks.","Norovirus: calicivirus, + ssRNA; low infectious dose, environmentally stable; vomiting predominant; outbreaks in hospitals, ships, schools.","Norovirus diagnosis: RT-PCR; control by hand washing with soap (alcohol less effective), bleach disinfection. Treatment of both: ORS, zinc."],"Rotavirus = 'Round wheel, Rotavac'; Norovirus = 'Nowhere safe (outbreaks)'."],
"Rotavirus (Reoviridae) is a non-enveloped virus (70 nm) with a triple-layered icosahedral capsid (wheel-like on EM — 'rota') enclosing 11 segments of double-stranded RNA; outer capsid proteins VP7 (G type) and VP4 (P type) define serotypes; reassortment occurs. It is the leading cause of severe dehydrating diarrhoea in children under 5 (peak 6–24 months), transmitted faeco-orally with very low infectious dose; infects mature enterocytes at villus tips, causing villous shortening, malabsorption and secretory diarrhoea mediated by non-structural protein NSP4 (viral enterotoxin) and enteric nervous system activation. Clinical: incubation 1–3 days; vomiting, fever, profuse watery diarrhoea for 3–8 days, dehydration; winter peaks. Diagnosis: stool antigen detection (ELISA, latex, ICT — VP6), electron microscopy, RNA PAGE (electropherotyping), RT-PCR, genotyping. Prevention: oral live vaccines (Rotavac, Rotasiil, Rotarix) in UIP at 6, 10, 14 weeks. Norovirus (Caliciviridae) is a small (27–38 nm), non-enveloped, positive-sense ssRNA virus; genogroups GI, GII (GII.4 predominant); extremely contagious (infective dose < 20 particles), environmentally stable, resistant to alcohol and chlorine at low levels, with short-lived immunity and many genotypes. Transmission: faeco-oral, vomitus aerosols, contaminated food (shellfish, salads), water, surfaces; causes epidemic gastroenteritis in all age groups — outbreaks in cruise ships, hospitals, nursing homes, schools, restaurants. Clinical: incubation 12–48 hours; sudden projectile vomiting, watery diarrhoea, cramps, low-grade fever, resolves in 1–3 days. Diagnosis: RT-PCR (multiplex GI panels), antigen EIA (less sensitive), EM. Control: hand washing with soap and water, hypochlorite disinfection, exclusion of ill food handlers for 48 h after recovery. Treatment of both: oral rehydration solution and zinc; no antivirals.",
[["Comparison",[["Feature","Rotavirus","Norovirus"],["Family","Reoviridae","Caliciviridae"],["Genome","11 segments dsRNA","+ ssRNA"],["Age","Infants < 2 years","All ages"],["Pattern","Endemic, winter","Outbreaks"],["Predominant symptom","Watery diarrhoea","Vomiting"],["Diagnosis","Stool antigen, RT-PCR","RT-PCR"],["Vaccine","Yes (UIP)","No"]]]],
{c:"Rotavirus vaccination has reduced severe infant diarrhoea, while norovirus outbreaks are controlled by strict hand hygiene and disinfection; ORS remains the key treatment."});

Z("micro12",2,"m",["Poxviruses are the largest viruses, brick-shaped DNA viruses replicating in the cytoplasm; variola (smallpox) was eradicated in 1980, and mpox (monkeypox) is a related virus now spreading among humans.","Big brick-shaped viruses that build their own factories in the cytoplasm (Guarnieri bodies); smallpox was defeated by vaccination, mpox is its cousin.",["Smallpox: variola; centrifugal rash, all lesions same stage, deep-seated; last natural case 1977 (Somalia); eradicated by WHO 1980.","Mpox: orthopoxvirus; zoonotic (rodents) and human-to-human (close/sexual contact); fever, lymphadenopathy (distinguishes from smallpox), vesiculopustular rash incl. genital; WHO PHEIC 2022 and 2024.","Molluscum contagiosum: pearly umbilicated papules; molluscum bodies.","Lab: PCR (vesicle swab — gold standard), EM (brick-shaped), culture (CAM pocks); vaccines: vaccinia, MVA-BN; tecovirimat."],"Smallpox vs chickenpox: smallpox 'Same stage, centrifugal, deep'; chickenpox 'different stages, centripetal, superficial'."],
"Poxviridae are the largest and most complex viruses (250–300 × 200 nm), brick-shaped (orthopox) or ovoid (parapox), with a complex coat, lateral bodies and dumbbell-shaped core containing linear double-stranded DNA; uniquely among DNA viruses, they replicate in the cytoplasm, carrying their own DNA-dependent RNA polymerase, forming cytoplasmic inclusion (Guarnieri) bodies. Genera infecting humans: Orthopoxvirus (variola, vaccinia, cowpox, monkeypox/mpox, buffalopox in India), Parapoxvirus (orf, milker's nodules), Molluscipoxvirus (molluscum contagiosum), Yatapoxvirus (tanapox). Smallpox (variola major and minor), transmitted by respiratory droplets and contact, caused fever and a characteristic centrifugal rash (face and extremities, including palms and soles) progressing synchronously through macule, papule, vesicle, pustule and crust, deep-seated, leaving pitted scars; mortality 30%; eradicated globally through WHO intensified programme using vaccinia vaccine and surveillance–containment (last natural case Ali Maow Maalin, Somalia, 1977; India smallpox-free 1977; declared eradicated 1980) — possible because of human-only host, no carrier state, visible disease, effective heat-stable vaccine and lasting immunity. Mpox (monkeypox virus; clades I and II; reservoir rodents in Africa) spreads to humans by animal contact and between humans through close skin-to-skin, sexual contact, respiratory droplets and fomites; outbreaks spread globally in 2022 (clade IIb) and clade Ib in 2024 (both declared PHEIC). Clinical: fever, headache, marked lymphadenopathy (distinguishing from smallpox), rash progressing through stages, often genital/perianal; complications in children, pregnancy, advanced HIV. Molluscum contagiosum causes pearly, umbilicated papules (molluscum bodies — Henderson–Patterson), sexually transmitted in adults, extensive in HIV. Laboratory diagnosis: specimens from vesicle fluid, roof and crust; PCR (gold standard; clade typing); electron microscopy (brick-shaped virions); culture in cell lines or chick CAM (pocks); serology; handled in BSL-3. Prevention and treatment: vaccinia-based vaccines (ACAM2000, MVA-BN/Jynneos), tecovirimat, isolation.",
[["Smallpox vs chickenpox",[["Feature","Smallpox","Chickenpox"],["Rash distribution","Centrifugal (face, limbs, palms, soles)","Centripetal (trunk)"],["Stage of lesions","All same stage","Different stages together (pleomorphic)"],["Depth","Deep, firm","Superficial, dew-drop"],["Scars","Pitted","Rare"],["Prodrome","Severe","Mild"]]],
["Mpox features",["Zoonotic + human-to-human (sexual, close contact)","Fever, prominent lymphadenopathy","Vesiculopustular rash, often genital","PCR diagnosis; tecovirimat, MVA-BN vaccine"]]],
{c:"Smallpox eradication remains public health's greatest triumph; mpox shows how related orthopoxviruses can re-emerge, requiring PCR diagnosis, isolation and vaccination."});

Z("micro12",3,"m",["Human papillomavirus is a small DNA virus that infects skin and mucosa, causing warts and, for high-risk types (16, 18), cervical and other anogenital and throat cancers; it is preventable by vaccine.","HPV slips into the basal layer of skin through tiny cracks; low-risk types make harmless warts, high-risk types slowly reprogramme cells to become cancerous.",["Non-enveloped dsDNA papillomavirus; > 200 types.","Low-risk: 6, 11 — genital warts (condyloma acuminata), laryngeal papillomas; cutaneous: 1, 2, 4 — common/plantar warts.","High-risk: 16, 18 (70% cervical cancer), 31, 33, 45; oncoproteins E6 (p53) and E7 (Rb).","Diagnosis: Pap smear (koilocytes), HPV DNA testing, VIA; vaccines (quadrivalent, 9-valent, Cervavac — India) for girls 9–14 years."],"HPV 6 & 11 = warts; 16 & 18 = cancer."],
"Human papillomaviruses (Papillomaviridae) are small (55 nm), non-enveloped, icosahedral viruses with circular double-stranded DNA; more than 200 genotypes with tropism for squamous epithelium of skin (cutaneous types) or mucosa (mucosal types). The virus infects basal keratinocytes through microabrasions; viral replication is linked to epithelial differentiation, producing virions in superficial layers; koilocytes (cells with perinuclear halo and wrinkled nuclei) are characteristic. Transmission: direct contact, sexual intercourse, perinatal (laryngeal papillomatosis). Low-risk types: cutaneous warts — verruca vulgaris (HPV 2, 4), plantar warts (1), flat warts (3, 10); anogenital warts (condyloma acuminatum — HPV 6, 11), recurrent respiratory papillomatosis (6, 11). High-risk types (16, 18, 31, 33, 45, 52, 58): persistent infection leads to integration of viral DNA, overexpression of E6 (degrades p53 via E6AP) and E7 (binds Rb, releasing E2F), genomic instability and progression from CIN/SIL to invasive carcinoma of the cervix (HPV in virtually 100%), anus, vulva, vagina, penis and oropharynx. Epidermodysplasia verruciformis with HPV 5, 8 (skin cancer). Diagnosis: clinical (warts), cytology (Pap smear, liquid-based cytology — koilocytes, SIL), histology with p16 immunostaining, HPV DNA testing (PCR, hybrid capture; primary screening), visual inspection with acetic acid (VIA) in low-resource settings, colposcopy. Virus cannot be grown in routine culture. Prevention: HPV vaccines (VLP of L1 protein — bivalent 16/18, quadrivalent 6/11/16/18, nonavalent; Indian quadrivalent Cervavac) given to girls 9–14 years (2 doses), now in India's programme; screening; condoms (partial). Treatment: warts — cryotherapy, podophyllin, imiquimod, surgery; precancer — LEEP, ablation.",
[["Types and diseases",[["HPV types","Disease"],["1, 2, 4","Plantar and common warts"],["3, 10","Flat warts"],["6, 11","Genital warts, laryngeal papillomatosis"],["16, 18, 31, 33, 45","Cervical, anal, oropharyngeal, penile, vulval cancer"],["5, 8","Epidermodysplasia verruciformis"]]],
["Oncogenesis",["Viral DNA integration (disrupts E2)","E6 → p53 degradation","E7 → Rb inactivation","Persistent infection + cofactors (smoking, HIV) → CIN → cancer"]]],
{c:"HPV is the necessary cause of cervical cancer; vaccination of adolescent girls and HPV-based screening can make cervical cancer a rare disease."});

Z("micro12",4,"m",["Hepatitis C is a blood-borne RNA virus that usually becomes chronic and leads to cirrhosis and liver cancer but is now curable with oral drugs; hepatitis D needs HBV to survive; hepatitis E spreads by contaminated water and is dangerous in pregnancy.","HCV is a silent long-term resident; HDV is a parasite of HBV (it borrows HBV's coat); HEV is a waterborne visitor like HAV but deadly for pregnant women.",["HCV: flavivirus, + ssRNA, 6 genotypes (3 commonest in India); blood transfusion, IV drugs, unsafe injections, dialysis; 70–80% chronic; no vaccine; DAAs (sofosbuvir + velpatasvir) cure > 95%.","HDV: defective RNA virus with HBsAg envelope; co-infection or superinfection of HBV → severe/fulminant hepatitis, rapid cirrhosis; prevented by HBV vaccine.","HEV: hepevirus, + ssRNA, faeco-oral (waterborne epidemics — Delhi 1955–56); acute self-limited; 20–30% mortality in third-trimester pregnancy; chronic in immunosuppressed (genotype 3).","Lab: anti-HCV ELISA → HCV RNA PCR; anti-HDV IgM/IgG, HDV RNA; anti-HEV IgM, HEV RNA."],"HCV = 'Chronic, Cirrhosis, Cure (DAA)'; HDV = 'Defective, needs B'; HEV = 'Enteric, Expectant mothers die'."],
"Hepatitis C virus (Flaviviridae, Hepacivirus) is an enveloped virus with positive-sense ssRNA; high mutation rate produces quasispecies and 6+ genotypes (genotype 3 most common in India, then 1). Transmission: parenteral — transfusion (before screening), IV drug use, unsafe injections and reuse of syringes, haemodialysis, needle-stick injuries, tattooing; less commonly sexual and perinatal. Clinical: acute infection usually asymptomatic; 70–85% become chronically infected; chronic hepatitis leads to cirrhosis (20% over 20–30 years) and hepatocellular carcinoma; extrahepatic — mixed cryoglobulinaemia, MPGN, porphyria cutanea tarda, lichen planus, B-cell lymphoma, insulin resistance. Diagnosis: anti-HCV antibody (ELISA, rapid tests) for screening; HCV RNA (RT-PCR) confirms active infection and monitors therapy (sustained virological response); HCV core antigen; genotyping; assessment of fibrosis. Treatment: pan-genotypic direct-acting antivirals (sofosbuvir + velpatasvir/daclatasvir) for 12 weeks cure over 95%; no vaccine. Hepatitis D virus (Deltavirus) is a defective, circular negative-sense ssRNA virus requiring HBsAg as its envelope (helper virus HBV); transmitted parenterally and sexually. Co-infection (simultaneous with HBV) — usually acute, self-limited, higher risk of fulminant hepatitis; superinfection of a chronic HBV carrier — severe acute hepatitis and rapid progression to chronic hepatitis D and cirrhosis. Diagnosis: anti-HDV IgM/IgG, HDV RNA, HDAg; prevented by HBV vaccination. Hepatitis E virus (Hepeviridae) is a small non-enveloped (quasi-enveloped in blood) positive-sense ssRNA virus; genotypes 1 and 2 (humans; waterborne epidemics in India — Delhi 1955–56, Kanpur) and 3, 4 (zoonotic — pigs; chronic infection in transplant recipients). Transmission: faeco-oral via contaminated water. Clinical: acute self-limited hepatitis in young adults; no chronicity in normal hosts (genotypes 1, 2); fulminant hepatic failure with 20–30% mortality in pregnant women in the third trimester; extrahepatic neurological manifestations. Diagnosis: anti-HEV IgM (acute), IgG, HEV RNA by RT-PCR in stool and serum. Prevention: safe water and sanitation; HEV vaccine (Hecolin, China).",
[["Comparison",[["Feature","HCV","HDV","HEV"],["Genome","+ ssRNA","Circular − ssRNA (defective)","+ ssRNA"],["Family","Flaviviridae","Deltavirus","Hepeviridae"],["Transmission","Parenteral","Parenteral (with HBV)","Faeco-oral (water)"],["Chronicity","70–85%","Common in superinfection","No (except immunosuppressed)"],["Special","Cirrhosis, HCC, cured by DAAs","Needs HBsAg; severe hepatitis","Pregnancy mortality 20–30%"],["Diagnosis","Anti-HCV, HCV RNA","Anti-HDV, HDV RNA","Anti-HEV IgM, HEV RNA"],["Vaccine","None","HBV vaccine protects","Not in India"]]]],
{c:"HCV is a curable cause of chronic liver disease; HDV is preventable by HBV vaccination; HEV causes waterborne epidemics with high mortality in pregnancy."});

Z("micro12",5,"m",["SARS-CoV-2 is an enveloped RNA coronavirus that caused the COVID-19 pandemic, spreading through respiratory droplets and aerosols and causing illness ranging from mild cold-like symptoms to severe pneumonia and multi-organ failure.","The spike protein is a key fitting the ACE2 lock on our cells; changes in the key (variants like Delta, Omicron) let the virus spread faster or escape immunity.",["Betacoronavirus, + ssRNA (~30 kb), spike (S) protein binds ACE2; TMPRSS2 helps entry.","Spread: droplets, aerosols, fomites; incubation 2–14 days; asymptomatic transmission.","Clinical: fever, cough, anosmia, fatigue; severe — pneumonia, ARDS, cytokine storm, thrombosis; risk — elderly, diabetes, obesity; complications — mucormycosis (India), MIS-C, long COVID.","Lab: RT-PCR (nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal swab — gold standard), rapid antigen test, serology (antibodies), genomic sequencing (INSACOG); vaccines (Covishield, Covaxin, mRNA)."],"COVID entry: 'Spike → ACE2 + TMPRSS2'."],
"SARS-CoV-2 (Coronaviridae, Betacoronavirus) is an enveloped, spherical (60–140 nm) virus with crown-like spike projections and a large (~30 kb) positive-sense ssRNA genome encoding structural proteins — spike (S; receptor binding domain binds ACE2), envelope (E), membrane (M), nucleocapsid (N) — and non-structural proteins including RNA-dependent RNA polymerase (target of remdesivir) and 3CL protease (target of nirmatrelvir). Emerged in Wuhan, China, December 2019 (likely zoonotic, bats); WHO declared a pandemic in March 2020. Variants of concern — Alpha, Beta, Gamma, Delta (severe second wave in India 2021), Omicron and sublineages (high transmissibility, immune escape). Transmission: respiratory droplets, aerosols (especially indoors), contaminated surfaces; pre-symptomatic and asymptomatic transmission; R0 2–3 (higher for Omicron). Pathogenesis: entry into respiratory epithelial cells via ACE2 with TMPRSS2 priming; diffuse alveolar damage; dysregulated immune response (cytokine storm — IL-6), endothelial injury, hypercoagulability, lymphopenia. Clinical spectrum: asymptomatic, mild (fever, dry cough, sore throat, myalgia, fatigue, anosmia, ageusia, diarrhoea), moderate pneumonia, severe disease (hypoxia, ARDS), critical (shock, multi-organ failure, thrombosis); risk factors — age, diabetes, hypertension, obesity, chronic diseases, immunosuppression; complications — secondary infections including COVID-associated mucormycosis (large outbreak in India with steroid use and diabetes), MIS-C in children, long COVID. Laboratory diagnosis: specimen — nasopharyngeal and oropharyngeal swabs in viral transport medium (or saliva), lower respiratory samples in severe cases; real-time RT-PCR (gold standard; targets E, RdRp, N, ORF1ab genes; Ct value), TrueNat, CBNAAT; rapid antigen tests (N protein; quick but less sensitive); antibody tests (IgM/IgG against S/N — serosurveys, not acute diagnosis); whole genome sequencing for variants (INSACOG network in India); supportive — lymphopenia, raised CRP, D-dimer, ferritin, IL-6, CT chest (ground-glass opacities). Prevention: vaccines — Covishield (ChAdOx1 viral vector), Covaxin (inactivated), Corbevax (protein subunit), mRNA vaccines; masks, ventilation, hand hygiene, distancing, isolation and quarantine. Treatment: supportive oxygen, corticosteroids (dexamethasone) for hypoxic patients, anticoagulation, antivirals (remdesivir, nirmatrelvir–ritonavir, molnupiravir), IL-6 blockers (tocilizumab).",
[["Laboratory diagnosis",[["Test","Use"],["RT-PCR","Gold standard diagnosis"],["Rapid antigen test","Quick screening, high viral load"],["TrueNat, CBNAAT","Point-of-care molecular"],["Serology","Past infection, serosurveys"],["Genome sequencing","Variant surveillance"]]],
["Clinical severity",["Mild: URTI symptoms, anosmia","Moderate: pneumonia, SpO2 ≥ 94%","Severe: SpO2 < 94%, RR > 30","Critical: ARDS, shock, multi-organ failure"]]],
{c:"SARS-CoV-2 demonstrated the power of rapid molecular diagnostics, genomic surveillance and vaccines; RT-PCR remains the diagnostic standard."});

Z("micro12",6,"m",["Emerging infections are new or re-appearing diseases; important ones include Nipah (bats, encephalitis, Kerala), Zika (Aedes, microcephaly), chikungunya (Aedes, severe joint pain), Kyasanur forest disease (ticks, Karnataka) and Ebola (haemorrhagic fever, Africa).","Many come from animals through new contact (bats, monkeys, ticks) or spread by mosquitoes into new areas; surveillance and quick isolation stop them.",["Nipah: paramyxovirus; fruit bats (Pteropus) → humans (date palm sap, pigs, person-to-person); encephalitis, respiratory illness; 40–75% mortality; Kerala outbreaks (2018 onwards); RT-PCR (BSL-4).","Zika & chikungunya: Aedes-borne; Zika — mild illness, microcephaly, GBS; chikungunya (togavirus) — fever with severe, prolonged polyarthralgia.","KFD (monkey fever): flavivirus; Haemaphysalis ticks, monkeys; Karnataka Western Ghats; haemorrhagic fever, neurological phase; KFD vaccine.","Ebola/Marburg: filoviruses; bats reservoir; contact with body fluids; haemorrhagic fever, high mortality; RT-PCR (BSL-4); vaccine rVSV-ZEBOV."],"Nipah = 'Bats, Kerala, encephalitis'; KFD = 'Ticks & monkeys, Karnataka'."],
"Emerging infections are those newly appearing in a population or rapidly increasing in incidence or geographic range; re-emerging infections are known diseases returning after decline. Drivers: zoonotic spillover (deforestation, wildlife contact), urbanisation, climate change, international travel and trade, microbial adaptation, breakdown of public health. Nipah virus (Paramyxoviridae, Henipavirus) has fruit bats (Pteropus) as natural reservoir; transmission to humans by consuming raw date palm sap contaminated by bat saliva/urine (Bangladesh), via pigs (Malaysia 1998–99), and person-to-person (nosocomial; Siliguri 2001, Nadia 2007, Kerala 2018, 2019, 2021, 2023, 2024). Clinical: fever, headache, acute encephalitis, respiratory distress; case fatality 40–75%; relapse encephalitis. Diagnosis: RT-PCR on throat swab, CSF, urine, blood; IgM ELISA (BSL-4 at NIV Pune). No vaccine; monoclonal antibody (m102.4) and remdesivir experimental; infection control and contact tracing are key. Zika virus (Flaviviridae) transmitted by Aedes aegypti, also sexually and vertically; usually mild febrile illness with rash, conjunctivitis, arthralgia; congenital Zika syndrome (microcephaly) and Guillain–Barré syndrome; outbreaks in India (Rajasthan, Kerala, UP). Chikungunya virus (Togaviridae, Alphavirus) transmitted by Aedes; sudden high fever with severe, often prolonged incapacitating polyarthralgia ('that which bends up'), rash; re-emerged in India in 2006. Diagnosis: RT-PCR (first week), IgM ELISA. Kyasanur forest disease (KFD, monkey fever; Flaviviridae) was first identified in 1957 in Shimoga, Karnataka; maintained by Haemaphysalis spinigera ticks with monkeys (langurs, macaques) as amplifying hosts; spreading in Western Ghats (Karnataka, Kerala, Goa, Maharashtra); biphasic illness with haemorrhagic fever followed by meningoencephalitis; KFD formalin-inactivated vaccine. Ebola and Marburg viruses (Filoviridae) — filamentous negative-sense RNA viruses with fruit bat reservoirs; spread by direct contact with blood and body fluids; viral haemorrhagic fever with high mortality; West Africa epidemic 2014–16; diagnosis by RT-PCR in BSL-4; rVSV-ZEBOV vaccine and monoclonal antibodies (Inmazeb, Ebanga). Others: avian influenza H5N1, MERS-CoV, Crimean–Congo haemorrhagic fever (Gujarat), SARS-CoV-2, mpox, Candida auris.",
[["Emerging infections",[["Disease","Virus family","Reservoir/vector","Key feature","India"],["Nipah","Paramyxovirus","Fruit bats","Encephalitis, high mortality","Kerala, West Bengal"],["Zika","Flavivirus","Aedes","Microcephaly, GBS","Sporadic outbreaks"],["Chikungunya","Togavirus","Aedes","Severe polyarthralgia","Endemic"],["KFD","Flavivirus","Ticks, monkeys","Haemorrhagic fever + encephalitis","Western Ghats"],["Ebola","Filovirus","Bats","Haemorrhagic fever","Not endemic"]]],
["Control",["Surveillance (IDSP, NIV), early detection, BSL-3/4 diagnostics","Isolation, PPE, contact tracing","Vector control (Aedes, ticks)","Vaccines where available (KFD, Ebola)","One Health approach"]]],
{c:"Emerging infections threaten global health; rapid laboratory diagnosis, infection control and One Health surveillance are the main defences."});

Z("micro12",7,"m",["Prions are infectious proteins without any nucleic acid; misfolded prion protein converts normal protein into the abnormal form, causing fatal brain degenerations like Creutzfeldt–Jakob disease.","A 'bad apple' protein that folds the wrong way and makes neighbouring good proteins fold wrongly too, until the brain becomes spongy.",["Normal PrPc (α-helix) → abnormal PrPsc (β-sheet), protease-resistant, aggregates.","Diseases: sporadic CJD (commonest), familial CJD, GSS, fatal familial insomnia, iatrogenic CJD (dura grafts, corneal grafts, growth hormone), variant CJD (mad cow — BSE), kuru (cannibalism).","Pathology: spongiform change, neuronal loss, gliosis, no inflammation; amyloid plaques (kuru, vCJD — florid plaques).","Diagnosis: rapidly progressive dementia, myoclonus; EEG (periodic sharp waves), MRI, CSF 14-3-3 and RT-QuIC; resistant to standard sterilisation (needs autoclave 134 °C + NaOH)."],"Prion = 'Protein only, Spongy brain, Sterilisation-resistant'."],
"Prions (proteinaceous infectious particles, Prusiner — Nobel 1997) are infectious agents composed solely of an abnormal isoform of a host-encoded protein, lacking nucleic acid. Normal cellular prion protein (PrPc, encoded by PRNP gene on chromosome 20; mainly α-helical; protease-sensitive) is converted into the scrapie isoform PrPsc (rich in β-pleated sheets; protease-resistant, insoluble), which acts as a template converting more PrPc into PrPsc, forming aggregates and amyloid that cause neuronal death. Properties: no immune response or inflammation, long incubation (years to decades), uniformly fatal; resistant to heat, formalin, UV and ionising radiation, standard autoclaving; inactivated by autoclaving at 134 °C for 18 minutes, 1 N NaOH, or sodium hypochlorite. Human prion diseases (transmissible spongiform encephalopathies): sporadic Creutzfeldt–Jakob disease (85%; age 55–75; rapidly progressive dementia, myoclonus, ataxia, visual signs; death within a year), familial/genetic (familial CJD, Gerstmann–Sträussler–Scheinker syndrome, fatal familial insomnia — PRNP mutations), acquired — iatrogenic CJD (contaminated neurosurgical instruments, depth electrodes, dura mater and corneal grafts, cadaveric pituitary growth hormone), variant CJD (younger patients, psychiatric onset; from BSE-contaminated beef, also blood transfusion), kuru (Fore people of Papua New Guinea; ritual cannibalism). Animal diseases: scrapie (sheep), bovine spongiform encephalopathy (mad cow disease), chronic wasting disease (deer). Pathology: spongiform change (vacuolation of neuropil), neuronal loss, astrocytic gliosis, absent inflammation; PrPsc deposition; kuru and florid plaques (vCJD). Diagnosis: clinical features; EEG — periodic sharp wave complexes (sCJD); MRI — cortical ribboning, basal ganglia (caudate, putamen) hyperintensity, pulvinar sign (vCJD); CSF — 14-3-3 protein, tau, RT-QuIC (real-time quaking-induced conversion; highly specific); tonsil biopsy (vCJD); brain biopsy/autopsy with immunohistochemistry and Western blot for protease-resistant PrP; PRNP gene sequencing. No treatment; prevention by special decontamination of instruments, single-use instruments, recombinant growth hormone, banning of meat-and-bone meal feed.",
[["Human prion diseases",[["Type","Disease","Cause"],["Sporadic","sCJD","Spontaneous conversion"],["Genetic","fCJD, GSS, fatal familial insomnia","PRNP mutation"],["Acquired","Iatrogenic CJD","Grafts, instruments, hGH"],["Acquired","Variant CJD","BSE beef, transfusion"],["Acquired","Kuru","Ritual cannibalism"]]],
["Decontamination",["Standard autoclaving inadequate","Autoclave at 134 °C for 18 min","1 N NaOH or 20,000 ppm hypochlorite","Discard instruments used in suspected cases"]]],
{c:"Prions challenge classical microbiology as infectious proteins; diagnosis rests on clinical, EEG, MRI and RT-QuIC findings, and prevention depends on special sterilisation measures."});

Z("micro13",0,"m",["Healthcare-associated infections (HAIs) are infections acquired in hospital that were not present at admission; the main device- and procedure-associated types are CAUTI, CLABSI, VAP and surgical site infection.","Each device is a bridge across the body's defences — catheters, lines, tubes and incisions let germs walk in; bundles of simple preventive steps block these bridges.",["HAI: appears ≥ 48 h after admission (or within 30 days of surgery, 90 days for implants).","CAUTI: urinary catheter > 2 days; E. coli, Klebsiella, Enterococcus, Candida; bundle — avoid, aseptic insertion, closed drainage, early removal.","CLABSI: central line; CoNS, S. aureus, Klebsiella, Candida; bundle — hand hygiene, maximal barrier, chlorhexidine skin prep, avoid femoral, daily review.","VAP: ventilated > 48 h; Pseudomonas, Acinetobacter, Klebsiella, MRSA; bundle — head elevation 30–45°, daily sedation hold, oral chlorhexidine, subglottic suction. SSI: S. aureus; bundle — prophylactic antibiotic within 60 min, clipping not shaving, glucose control, normothermia."],"HAI big four: 'CAUTI, CLABSI, VAP, SSI' — prevented by bundles."],
"Healthcare-associated (nosocomial) infections are infections acquired by patients while receiving care in a healthcare facility that were neither present nor incubating at admission, usually appearing 48 hours or more after admission (up to 30 days after surgery, 90 days with implants); they also include occupational infections of staff. They affect 5–10% of hospitalised patients (higher in ICUs and developing countries), increase morbidity, mortality, length of stay, costs and antimicrobial resistance. Sources: endogenous (patient's own flora) or exogenous (cross-infection via hands of staff, equipment, environment, air, water, food). Common pathogens: Gram-negatives (Klebsiella pneumoniae, E. coli, Acinetobacter baumannii, Pseudomonas aeruginosa — often MDR, carbapenem-resistant), Gram-positives (MRSA, CoNS, Enterococcus/VRE), Candida (including C. auris), C. difficile, viruses (influenza, norovirus, RSV, SARS-CoV-2, HBV, HCV). Risk factors: extremes of age, immunosuppression, severe illness, invasive devices, surgery, prolonged hospitalisation, antibiotic exposure. Major device-associated HAIs (CDC/NHSN definitions): catheter-associated urinary tract infection (CAUTI; commonest HAI; indwelling urinary catheter > 2 days; biofilm formation; prevention — insert only when indicated, aseptic technique, closed drainage, bag below bladder level, daily review and early removal); central line-associated bloodstream infection (CLABSI; laboratory-confirmed bloodstream infection with central line > 2 days; prevention — insertion bundle: hand hygiene, maximal sterile barrier precautions, 2% chlorhexidine–alcohol skin antisepsis, avoid femoral site, ultrasound guidance; maintenance bundle: hub disinfection, dressing care, daily review of necessity); ventilator-associated pneumonia (VAP; pneumonia in patients ventilated > 48 hours; aspiration of colonised oropharyngeal secretions; prevention — head-of-bed elevation 30–45°, daily sedation interruption and readiness to extubate, oral care with chlorhexidine, subglottic secretion drainage, peptic ulcer and DVT prophylaxis, avoiding unnecessary circuit changes); surgical site infection (SSI; superficial incisional, deep incisional, organ/space; S. aureus most common; prevention — preoperative bath, hair clipping (not shaving), appropriate prophylactic antibiotic within 60 minutes before incision, skin preparation with chlorhexidine–alcohol, glycaemic control, normothermia, sterile technique, OT ventilation). Others: C. difficile infection, hospital-acquired pneumonia, bloodstream infections, gastroenteritis, skin infections, blood-borne viral infections. Laboratory role: surveillance (device-days, infection rates per 1000 device-days), cultures with AST, outbreak investigation and typing, antibiograms, environmental sampling when indicated. Prevention and control: hospital infection control committee and team, hand hygiene, standard and transmission-based precautions, care bundles, sterilisation and disinfection, environmental cleaning, biomedical waste management, antimicrobial stewardship, staff vaccination and education.",
[["Device-associated HAIs",[["HAI","Organisms","Key prevention bundle"],["CAUTI","E. coli, Klebsiella, Enterococcus, Pseudomonas, Candida","Avoid catheter, aseptic insertion, closed drainage, early removal"],["CLABSI","CoNS, S. aureus, Klebsiella, Enterococcus, Candida","Maximal barrier, chlorhexidine, avoid femoral, daily review"],["VAP","Pseudomonas, Acinetobacter, Klebsiella, MRSA","Head up 30–45°, sedation vacation, oral care, subglottic suction"],["SSI","S. aureus, CoNS, Enterobacteriaceae","Antibiotic prophylaxis ≤ 60 min, clipping, normothermia, glucose control"]]],
["Sources and routes",["Endogenous flora","Hands of healthcare workers (most important route)","Contaminated equipment, environment, water, air","Invasive devices and procedures"]],
["Surveillance",["Device-associated infection rate = (infections / device-days) × 1000","Antibiograms, outbreak detection, molecular typing","Feedback to clinical teams"]]],
{i:"HAIs are among the most common adverse events in healthcare and a major driver of antimicrobial resistance; most are preventable.",x:[["Infection control programme",["Hospital Infection Control Committee and infection control nurses","Hand hygiene compliance audits","Standard and transmission-based precautions","Sterilisation, disinfection, environmental cleaning","Biomedical waste management","Antimicrobial stewardship","Staff immunisation (HBV), needle-stick protocols"]]],f:"Patient risk + invasive device/procedure + resistant hospital flora\n → colonisation (hands, environment, biofilm) → infection (CAUTI, CLABSI, VAP, SSI)\nPrevention: hand hygiene + bundles + surveillance + stewardship",c:"Device-associated HAIs are largely preventable through evidence-based bundles, hand hygiene, surveillance and antimicrobial stewardship."});

Z("micro13",1,"m",["Hand hygiene is the single most effective way to prevent healthcare-associated infections; WHO defines five moments when hands must be cleaned and a standard sequence of steps.","Hands are the main vehicles carrying germs from patient to patient — cleaning them at the right moments breaks the chain.",["WHO 5 moments: before touching a patient; before clean/aseptic procedure; after body fluid exposure risk; after touching a patient; after touching patient surroundings.","Alcohol-based hand rub (20–30 s) preferred unless hands are visibly soiled; soap and water (40–60 s) for visible soiling, C. difficile, norovirus.","Steps: palm to palm, back of hands, interlaced fingers, backs of fingers, thumbs, fingertips in palms (± wrists).","Keep nails short, no rings/artificial nails; gloves do not replace hand hygiene."],"5 moments: 'Before patient, Before aseptic, After fluids, After patient, After surroundings'."],
"Hand hygiene refers to any action of hand cleansing — handwashing with soap and water, antiseptic handwash, or alcohol-based hand rub — and is the most important measure to prevent transmission of healthcare-associated pathogens. Hand flora: transient (acquired by contact, easily removed; responsible for cross-transmission — S. aureus, Gram-negative bacilli) and resident (deeper, CoNS, diphtheroids). WHO 'My 5 Moments for Hand Hygiene': (1) before touching a patient, (2) before a clean/aseptic procedure, (3) after body fluid exposure risk, (4) after touching a patient, (5) after touching patient surroundings. Methods: alcohol-based hand rub (60–80% ethanol or isopropanol with emollient; 20–30 seconds; preferred for routine use as it is faster, more effective and less damaging); handwashing with soap and water (40–60 seconds) when hands are visibly dirty or soiled with blood/body fluids, after using the toilet, and when exposure to spore-formers (C. difficile, Bacillus) or norovirus is suspected (alcohol is not sporicidal); surgical hand antisepsis (2–5 minutes with chlorhexidine or povidone-iodine scrub or alcohol rub). WHO technique (steps): apply product; rub palm to palm; right palm over left dorsum with interlaced fingers and vice versa; palm to palm with fingers interlaced; backs of fingers to opposing palms with fingers interlocked; rotational rubbing of thumbs; rotational rubbing of fingertips in palms; (wrists); rinse and dry with single-use towel (for washing). Supporting measures: short nails, no artificial nails or jewellery, skin care, availability of alcohol rub at point of care, education, compliance monitoring and feedback, gloves (not a substitute; hand hygiene before and after glove use). Multimodal strategy: system change, training, monitoring and feedback, reminders, institutional safety climate.",
[["WHO 5 moments",[["Moment","Purpose"],["1. Before touching a patient","Protect patient from germs on hands"],["2. Before clean/aseptic procedure","Prevent entry of germs into patient"],["3. After body fluid exposure risk","Protect self and environment"],["4. After touching a patient","Protect self and environment"],["5. After touching patient surroundings","Protect self and environment"]]],
["Rub vs wash",[["Feature","Alcohol rub","Soap and water"],["Duration","20–30 s","40–60 s"],["Use","Routine, hands not soiled","Visible soiling, C. difficile, norovirus"],["Spores","Not effective","Mechanical removal"]]]],
{c:"Consistent hand hygiene at the WHO five moments, mainly with alcohol-based rub, is the cornerstone of infection prevention."});

Z("micro13",2,"m",["Standard precautions are basic infection control measures applied to every patient regardless of diagnosis; transmission-based precautions (contact, droplet, airborne) are added for specific infections.","Standard precautions are seatbelts worn on every journey; transmission-based precautions are extra safety gear for specific risky roads.",["Standard: hand hygiene, PPE as needed, respiratory hygiene/cough etiquette, safe injection practices, sharps safety, environmental cleaning, equipment reprocessing, linen and waste handling.","Contact (MRSA, C. difficile, MDR GNB, scabies): gloves + gown, single room/cohort, dedicated equipment.","Droplet (> 5 µm; influenza, meningococcus, pertussis, diphtheria, mumps, rubella): surgical mask within 1–2 m, single room.","Airborne (< 5 µm; TB, measles, varicella, disseminated zoster, COVID-19 aerosol procedures): N95 respirator, airborne infection isolation room (negative pressure, ≥ 12 ACH)."],"Airborne 'MTV': Measles, TB, Varicella → N95 + negative pressure."],
"Standard precautions are a set of infection-prevention practices applied to all patients in all healthcare settings, based on the principle that all blood, body fluids, secretions, excretions (except sweat), non-intact skin and mucous membranes may contain transmissible agents. Components: hand hygiene; use of personal protective equipment (gloves, gowns, masks, eye protection) according to anticipated exposure; respiratory hygiene and cough etiquette; safe injection practices (one needle, one syringe, one patient; single-dose vials); safe handling of sharps (no recapping, puncture-proof containers); patient placement; cleaning and disinfection of the environment and patient-care equipment; safe handling of linen; biomedical waste management; post-exposure management. Transmission-based precautions are used in addition for patients known or suspected to be infected with pathogens spread by specific routes: contact precautions — for organisms spread by direct or indirect contact (MRSA, VRE, CRE and other MDR organisms, C. difficile, norovirus, rotavirus, scabies, herpes zoster localised in immunocompetent, wound infections): single room or cohorting, gloves and gown on entry, dedicated equipment, limited patient transport; droplet precautions — for large droplets (> 5 µm) travelling < 1–2 m (influenza, N. meningitidis, Hib, pertussis, diphtheria, mumps, rubella, mycoplasma, adenovirus, plague pneumonia): single room or spatial separation > 1 m, surgical mask when within 1–2 m, patient wears mask during transport; airborne precautions — for small droplet nuclei (< 5 µm) remaining suspended and spread widely (pulmonary/laryngeal TB, measles, varicella, disseminated zoster, aerosol-generating procedures in COVID-19 and other respiratory pathogens): airborne infection isolation room with negative pressure, ≥ 12 air changes per hour, door closed, N95/FFP2 respirator (fit-tested), non-immune staff not to enter for measles/varicella, patient masked during transport. Some infections need combinations (varicella — airborne + contact; SARS-CoV-2 — droplet/contact ± airborne; viral haemorrhagic fevers — enhanced contact/droplet).",
[["Transmission-based precautions",[["Type","Particle/route","Examples","Key measures"],["Contact","Direct/indirect contact","MRSA, CRE, C. difficile, scabies, norovirus","Gloves + gown, single room/cohort"],["Droplet","> 5 µm, < 1–2 m","Influenza, meningococcus, pertussis, diphtheria, mumps, rubella","Surgical mask, single room"],["Airborne","< 5 µm, long distance","TB, measles, varicella, disseminated zoster","N95, negative-pressure room (≥ 12 ACH)"]]],
["Standard precautions components",["Hand hygiene","PPE based on risk","Respiratory hygiene, cough etiquette","Safe injection and sharps handling","Environmental cleaning, equipment reprocessing","Linen and biomedical waste handling"]]],
{c:"Standard precautions for all patients plus transmission-based precautions tailored to the route of spread form the framework of hospital infection control."});

Z("micro13",3,"m",["A needle-stick injury exposes a healthcare worker to blood-borne viruses (HBV, HCV, HIV); immediate first aid, risk assessment and timely post-exposure prophylaxis prevent infection.","Act within minutes: wash, report, test the source, and start PEP within hours — the earlier, the better.",["Risk of transmission after percutaneous exposure: HBV 6–30% (if HBeAg+), HCV ~1.8%, HIV ~0.3%.","First aid: wash with soap and running water; do not squeeze, suck or use bleach; mucosa — flush with water/saline.","Report, document; assess exposure and source status (HIV, HBsAg, anti-HCV — rapid tests with consent); baseline tests of exposed worker.","HIV PEP: start within 2 h (up to 72 h), 28 days of TLD (tenofovir + lamivudine + dolutegravir); HBV: vaccine ± HBIG if non-immune; HCV: no PEP — follow-up and early treatment. Follow-up testing at 6 weeks, 3 and 6 months."],"Needle-stick: 'Wash, Report, Assess, PEP within 2 hours, Follow-up'."],
"Needle-stick and sharps injuries are the commonest occupational exposures to blood-borne pathogens among healthcare workers. Average risk of transmission after percutaneous exposure to infected blood: HBV 6–30% (higher if source HBeAg-positive), HCV about 1.8%, HIV about 0.3% (mucous membrane 0.09%). Factors increasing risk: deep injury, visible blood on device, hollow-bore needle used in artery or vein, high source viral load, no gloves. Prevention: universal/standard precautions, HBV vaccination of all healthcare workers (with anti-HBs check), safe sharps handling — no recapping, immediate disposal in puncture-proof containers, safety-engineered devices, training. Management after exposure: (1) immediate first aid — wash the wound with soap and running water; do not scrub, squeeze or suck the wound; do not apply bleach or strong antiseptics; for splashes on mucosa or eyes, irrigate with plenty of water or saline; remove contact lenses; (2) report to the designated officer/emergency and document details (date, time, device, depth, source); (3) assess exposure — type (percutaneous, mucous membrane, non-intact skin), volume and nature of fluid; categorise severity (NACO: mild, moderate, severe); (4) evaluate the source — HIV, HBsAg, anti-HCV status (rapid tests with consent and counselling; if unknown, treat as positive based on risk); (5) baseline testing of exposed person for HIV, HBsAg, anti-HBs, anti-HCV; (6) post-exposure prophylaxis — HIV PEP: start as early as possible, ideally within 2 hours and not later than 72 hours, using three-drug regimen TLD (tenofovir + lamivudine + dolutegravir) for 28 days; HBV: if exposed person is vaccinated with known anti-HBs ≥ 10 mIU/mL — no treatment; unvaccinated or non-responder — HBIG (0.06 mL/kg) within 24 hours plus HBV vaccine series; HCV: no PEP; monitor HCV RNA and treat early with DAAs if infection occurs; (7) counselling, safe sex and avoid blood donation during follow-up; (8) follow-up testing for HIV at 6 weeks, 3 months and 6 months; HCV at 6 weeks (RNA) and 6 months; HBV as indicated; monitor drug side effects.",
[["Transmission risk",[["Virus","Percutaneous risk","PEP available"],["HBV","6–30%","HBIG + vaccine"],["HCV","~1.8%","No (early DAA if infected)"],["HIV","~0.3%","Yes — TLD for 28 days"]]],
["Steps",["Immediate first aid (wash, do not squeeze)","Report and document","Assess exposure and source","Baseline tests of HCW","Start PEP within 2 h (≤ 72 h)","Follow-up at 6 weeks, 3 and 6 months"]]],
{c:"Prompt first aid, reporting, source testing and PEP within hours, together with HBV vaccination and sharps safety, protect healthcare workers from blood-borne infections."});

Z("micro13",4,"m",["Antimicrobial resistance is the ability of microbes to survive drugs that once killed them; bacteria resist by destroying the drug, changing its target, blocking its entry or pumping it out.","Bacteria have four main defence tricks: scissors to cut the drug (enzymes), new locks (altered targets), closed doors (reduced permeability) and bilge pumps (efflux).",["Enzymatic inactivation: β-lactamases (ESBL, AmpC, carbapenemases — KPC, NDM, OXA-48), aminoglycoside-modifying enzymes, chloramphenicol acetyltransferase.","Target alteration: PBP2a (MRSA), D-Ala-D-Lac (VRE), rpoB mutation (rifampicin), gyrase mutations (fluoroquinolones), rRNA methylation (macrolides — erm).","Reduced permeability (porin loss) and efflux pumps (tetracycline, fluoroquinolones, multidrug).","Spread: mutation (vertical) and horizontal gene transfer (plasmids, transposons, integrons); drivers — misuse in humans and animals, poor infection control."],"AMR mechanisms 'D-T-P-E': Destroy, Target change, Permeability ↓, Efflux."],
"Antimicrobial resistance (AMR) is the ability of microorganisms to withstand antimicrobial agents to which they were previously susceptible. Types: intrinsic (natural — Pseudomonas to many drugs; Gram-negatives to vancomycin; Mycoplasma to β-lactams; Enterococcus to cephalosporins) and acquired (by mutation or gene acquisition). Genetic basis: chromosomal mutations (vertical transmission; e.g., rpoB in M. tuberculosis — rifampicin, gyrA/parC — fluoroquinolones, 23S rRNA — linezolid) and horizontal gene transfer of resistance genes carried on plasmids (R factors), transposons, integrons and gene cassettes by conjugation (most important), transduction and transformation. Biochemical mechanisms: (1) enzymatic inactivation or modification of the drug — β-lactamases (penicillinases, ESBLs — CTX-M, TEM, SHV; AmpC; carbapenemases — KPC (class A), NDM-1 (metallo-β-lactamase, class B; first described from New Delhi), OXA-48 (class D)), aminoglycoside-modifying enzymes (acetyltransferases, phosphotransferases, adenyltransferases), chloramphenicol acetyltransferase, erythromycin esterases; (2) alteration of the target — altered penicillin-binding proteins (PBP2a in MRSA via mecA; altered PBPs in penicillin-resistant pneumococcus), altered peptidoglycan precursor D-Ala-D-Lac (VanA/VanB in VRE), ribosomal methylation (erm — macrolide–lincosamide–streptogramin B resistance; 16S rRNA methylases — aminoglycosides), mutations in DNA gyrase/topoisomerase IV (quinolones), RNA polymerase (rifampicin), dihydropteroate synthase and dihydrofolate reductase (sulphonamides, trimethoprim), lipid A modification (mcr-1 plasmid — colistin); (3) decreased permeability — loss or mutation of outer membrane porins (OprD loss in Pseudomonas — imipenem); (4) active efflux pumps (tetracyclines — Tet proteins; multidrug efflux — MexAB-OprM in Pseudomonas, AcrAB-TolC in Enterobacteriaceae, NorA in S. aureus); (5) target protection (Qnr proteins protect gyrase), bypass pathways, overproduction of target, biofilm formation and persister cells (phenotypic tolerance). Drivers: overuse and misuse of antibiotics in humans (self-medication, OTC sale, incomplete courses, empirical broad-spectrum use), in animals and agriculture (growth promoters), poor infection control and sanitation, international travel, lack of new drugs. Detection: AST (disc diffusion, MIC, E-test, automated), phenotypic tests (ESBL — combined disc; carbapenemase — mCIM, Carba NP), molecular (PCR for mecA, vanA, blaNDM, blaKPC), whole genome sequencing. Consequences: treatment failure, increased mortality and costs. Control: antimicrobial stewardship, infection prevention, surveillance (ICMR AMRSN, WHO GLASS), National Action Plan on AMR (India 2017), One Health approach, Chennai Declaration, restriction of OTC sale (Schedule H1), vaccines, new drug development.",
[["Mechanisms",[["Mechanism","Example","Drugs affected"],["Enzymatic inactivation","β-lactamases (ESBL, NDM, KPC)","β-lactams"],["","Aminoglycoside-modifying enzymes","Aminoglycosides"],["Target alteration","PBP2a (MRSA)","β-lactams"],["","D-Ala-D-Lac (VRE)","Vancomycin"],["","erm methylation","Macrolides, clindamycin"],["","gyrA/parC mutation","Fluoroquinolones"],["","rpoB mutation","Rifampicin"],["Reduced permeability","Porin loss (OprD)","Carbapenems"],["Efflux pumps","MexAB-OprM, Tet pumps","Multiple, tetracyclines"],["Target protection","Qnr","Quinolones"]]],
["Genetic basis",["Intrinsic vs acquired","Mutation (chromosomal)","Horizontal transfer: plasmids (conjugation), transposons, integrons"]]],
{c:"Bacteria resist antibiotics by inactivation, target modification, reduced entry and efflux, spread mainly by plasmids; stewardship, infection control and One Health action are needed to contain AMR."});

Z("micro13",5,"m",["Antimicrobial stewardship is a coordinated programme to use antibiotics wisely — the right drug, dose, route and duration — to improve patient outcomes and slow resistance.","Antibiotics are a shared, limited resource; stewardship is like careful budget management so the medicines keep working for everyone.",["Goals: optimise outcomes, reduce resistance, adverse effects (C. difficile) and costs.","Core interventions: prospective audit and feedback, preauthorisation of restricted antibiotics, local guidelines and antibiograms, IV-to-oral switch, de-escalation based on cultures, dose optimisation, shortest effective duration.","Team: infectious disease physician, clinical microbiologist, clinical pharmacist, infection control, IT, administration.","Microbiology role: rapid accurate diagnosis, cascade/selective AST reporting, annual antibiogram, procalcitonin-guided stopping; India: ICMR AMSP, National Action Plan, AWaRe classification (Access, Watch, Reserve)."],"Stewardship '5 Ds': right Drug, Dose, Duration, De-escalation, Diagnosis."],
"Antimicrobial stewardship (AMS) refers to coordinated interventions designed to improve and measure the appropriate use of antimicrobial agents by promoting selection of the optimal drug regimen — including drug, dose, route and duration — to achieve the best clinical outcomes while minimising toxicity, emergence of resistance, C. difficile infection and costs. Core elements (CDC/ICMR): leadership commitment, accountability (physician leader), pharmacy expertise, action, tracking, reporting and education. Team: infectious disease physician, clinical microbiologist, clinical pharmacist, infection control practitioner, hospital epidemiologist, information technology support, administration. Strategies: front-end (preauthorisation/restriction of reserve antibiotics such as carbapenems, colistin, tigecycline, linezolid, daptomycin) and back-end (prospective audit and feedback — review of prescriptions at 48–72 hours, 'antibiotic time-out'); facility-specific treatment guidelines based on local antibiograms; education; order forms with indication and duration; de-escalation (narrowing spectrum after culture results) and discontinuation when infection is excluded; IV-to-oral switch; dose optimisation by PK/PD (extended infusion of β-lactams, therapeutic drug monitoring of vancomycin and aminoglycosides, renal dose adjustment); shortest effective duration (e.g., 5 days for community-acquired pneumonia, 7 days for uncomplicated Gram-negative bacteraemia); avoiding treatment of asymptomatic bacteriuria and colonisation; surgical prophylaxis limited to single dose or < 24 hours; diagnostic stewardship (appropriate specimen collection, rapid diagnostics — MALDI-TOF, multiplex PCR, procalcitonin). Role of microbiology laboratory: accurate and timely identification and AST, selective/cascade reporting of antibiotics, comments on contaminants, cumulative antibiograms (annual), alerts for MDR organisms, surveillance data. WHO AWaRe classification: Access (first-line, e.g., amoxicillin, co-trimoxazole, nitrofurantoin, doxycycline), Watch (higher resistance potential, e.g., ceftriaxone, ciprofloxacin, azithromycin, carbapenems — monitored), Reserve (last-resort, e.g., colistin, tigecycline, ceftazidime–avibactam, linezolid); target ≥ 60% Access use. Measurement: days of therapy (DOT) or defined daily doses (DDD) per 1000 patient-days, compliance with guidelines, resistance rates, C. difficile rates, outcomes. India: ICMR Antimicrobial Stewardship Programme guidelines, National Action Plan on AMR (2017), Kerala state action plan, NCDC treatment guidelines, red line campaign on antibiotic packaging.",
[["Strategies",[["Strategy","Example"],["Preauthorisation","Approval for carbapenems, colistin"],["Prospective audit & feedback","Review at 48–72 h"],["Guidelines","Based on local antibiogram"],["De-escalation","Narrow spectrum after culture"],["IV-to-oral switch","When clinically stable"],["Dose optimisation","Extended infusion, TDM"],["Duration","Shortest effective course"],["Diagnostic stewardship","Rapid tests, procalcitonin"]]],
["AWaRe",[["Group","Examples"],["Access","Amoxicillin, co-trimoxazole, nitrofurantoin, doxycycline"],["Watch","Ceftriaxone, ciprofloxacin, azithromycin, meropenem"],["Reserve","Colistin, tigecycline, ceftazidime–avibactam, linezolid"]]]],
{c:"Antimicrobial stewardship, backed by strong microbiology support and local data, preserves antibiotic effectiveness and improves patient outcomes."});

Z("micro13",6,"m",["ESBL, CRE, MRSA and VRE are 'superbugs': bacteria resistant to key antibiotic groups, mainly spreading in hospitals and making infections hard to treat.","Each superbug has a special resistance weapon: ESBL and carbapenemase enzymes chop up β-lactams; MRSA and VRE change the antibiotic's target.",["ESBL (E. coli, Klebsiella): hydrolyse penicillins, cephalosporins (incl. 3rd gen), aztreonam; inhibited by clavulanate; treat with carbapenems.","CRE (carbapenem-resistant Enterobacterales): carbapenemases KPC, NDM (New Delhi), OXA-48; treat with ceftazidime–avibactam (+ aztreonam for NDM), colistin, tigecycline.","MRSA: mecA → PBP2a; resistant to all β-lactams; vancomycin, linezolid. VRE (E. faecium): vanA/vanB → D-Ala-D-Lac; linezolid, daptomycin.","Detection: combined disc test (ESBL), mCIM/Carba NP (carbapenemase), cefoxitin disc (MRSA), vancomycin MIC/screen agar (VRE), PCR; control — contact precautions, hand hygiene, stewardship."],"NDM = New Delhi metallo-β-lactamase → CRE."],
"These multidrug-resistant organisms are priority pathogens in hospitals. Extended-spectrum β-lactamases (ESBLs) are plasmid-mediated class A β-lactamases (CTX-M — most common in India, TEM, SHV variants) produced mainly by E. coli and Klebsiella pneumoniae; they hydrolyse penicillins, first- to fourth-generation cephalosporins and aztreonam (not cephamycins or carbapenems) and are inhibited by clavulanic acid, sulbactam and tazobactam; plasmids often carry co-resistance to aminoglycosides, fluoroquinolones and co-trimoxazole. Detection: screening with ceftazidime/cefotaxime/ceftriaxone, phenotypic confirmatory combined disc test (≥ 5 mm increase in zone with clavulanate) or double-disc synergy, automated systems, PCR. Treatment: carbapenems (drug of choice for serious infections), piperacillin–tazobactam in selected cases, nitrofurantoin/fosfomycin for cystitis. Carbapenem-resistant Enterobacterales (CRE) are resistant to carbapenems mainly through carbapenemases — KPC (class A, serine), NDM-1 and other metallo-β-lactamases (class B; zinc-dependent; inhibited by EDTA; not by avibactam; NDM is widespread in India), OXA-48-like (class D) — or through ESBL/AmpC plus porin loss. Detection: carbapenem MIC, modified carbapenem inactivation method (mCIM) with EDTA (eCIM), Carba NP test, immunochromatographic lateral flow tests, PCR. Treatment: ceftazidime–avibactam (KPC, OXA-48), ceftazidime–avibactam plus aztreonam (NDM), meropenem–vaborbactam, colistin/polymyxin B, tigecycline, fosfomycin, aminoglycosides, cefiderocol. Carbapenem-resistant Acinetobacter and Pseudomonas are also critical priorities. Methicillin-resistant S. aureus (MRSA) carries mecA on SCCmec encoding PBP2a, conferring resistance to all β-lactams except ceftaroline; detected by cefoxitin disc diffusion, oxacillin screen agar, PBP2a latex, mecA PCR, chromogenic agar; treated with vancomycin, teicoplanin, linezolid, daptomycin. Vancomycin-resistant enterococci (VRE; mostly E. faecium) carry vanA (high-level resistance to vancomycin and teicoplanin; plasmid/transposon) or vanB (vancomycin only) operons that replace D-Ala-D-Ala with D-Ala-D-Lac, reducing vancomycin binding; risk factors — prolonged hospitalisation, ICU, prior vancomycin and cephalosporins; detected by vancomycin MIC, vancomycin screen agar (6 µg/mL), PCR; treated with linezolid, daptomycin, tigecycline. Control: active surveillance screening (rectal swabs for CRE/VRE, nasal for MRSA) in high-risk units, contact precautions, cohorting, hand hygiene, environmental cleaning, decolonisation (MRSA), antimicrobial stewardship.",
[["Superbugs",[["Organism","Mechanism","Detection","Treatment"],["ESBL (E. coli, Klebsiella)","CTX-M, TEM, SHV β-lactamases","Combined disc test","Carbapenems"],["CRE","KPC, NDM, OXA-48 carbapenemases","mCIM/eCIM, Carba NP, PCR","Ceftazidime–avibactam (± aztreonam), colistin"],["MRSA","mecA → PBP2a","Cefoxitin disc, PCR","Vancomycin, linezolid"],["VRE","vanA/vanB → D-Ala-D-Lac","Vancomycin MIC/screen agar","Linezolid, daptomycin"]]],
["Control",["Active surveillance cultures","Contact precautions and cohorting","Hand hygiene and environmental cleaning","Antimicrobial stewardship","Decolonisation (MRSA)"]]],
{c:"ESBL, CRE, MRSA and VRE limit treatment options; laboratory detection with infection control and stewardship are essential to curb their spread."});

Z("micro13",7,"m",["Biomedical waste is waste generated during diagnosis, treatment or research on humans or animals; India's BMW Management Rules (2016) require segregation at source into colour-coded containers and proper treatment and disposal.","Sort it right at the start: each colour bin leads the waste to the correct treatment — burning, autoclaving, shredding or deep burial.",["Yellow: anatomical waste, soiled dressings, expired medicines, chemical waste, microbiology cultures (pre-treated) → incineration/plasma pyrolysis/deep burial.","Red: contaminated recyclable plastics (tubing, catheters, IV sets, syringes without needles, gloves) → autoclave/microwave + shredding → recycling.","White (translucent, puncture-proof): sharps — needles, blades → autoclave + shredding/encapsulation.","Blue (cardboard box): glassware, metallic implants → disinfection, recycling. Microbiology: cultures and stocks pre-treated on site (autoclave) before yellow bag; bar-coding, no chlorinated plastic bags."],"BMW colours: 'Yellow = burn, Red = recycle plastic, White = sharps, Blue = glass'."],
"Biomedical waste (BMW) is any waste generated during diagnosis, treatment or immunisation of human beings or animals, or in research or production and testing of biologicals, and in health camps. About 10–25% of healthcare waste is hazardous (infectious, sharps, chemical, pharmaceutical, radioactive); the rest is general waste. Risks: injuries and infections (HBV, HCV, HIV) to healthcare and waste workers, environmental pollution (dioxins from improper burning), reuse of syringes. India's Bio-Medical Waste Management Rules, 2016 (amended 2018, 2019) mandate segregation at source into colour-coded, labelled, non-chlorinated bags and containers, pre-treatment of laboratory and highly infectious waste, storage not exceeding 48 hours, transport by authorised vehicles to Common Bio-medical Waste Treatment Facilities, bar-code tracking, training and immunisation of staff, record-keeping and annual reports. Categories: Yellow — (a) human anatomical waste, (b) animal anatomical waste, (c) soiled waste (dressings, cotton, plaster casts, blood bags), (d) expired/discarded medicines, (e) chemical waste, (f) chemical liquid waste (separate collection, pre-treatment), (g) discarded linen/mattresses with body fluids, (h) microbiology, biotechnology and clinical laboratory waste (cultures, stocks, specimens, blood bags, live/attenuated vaccines) — pre-treated by autoclaving/microwaving on site (per WHO/NACO guidelines) then sent for incineration; treatment — incineration, plasma pyrolysis or deep burial (in rural areas). Red — contaminated recyclable waste: tubing, bottles, IV sets, catheters, urine bags, syringes without needles, vacutainers with needles cut, gloves; treatment — autoclaving or microwaving/hydroclaving followed by shredding, then recycling (not landfill). White (translucent, puncture-, leak- and tamper-proof containers) — waste sharps including needles, syringes with fixed needles, scalpels, blades, metal sharps; treatment — autoclaving/dry heat sterilisation followed by shredding or mutilation or encapsulation. Blue (puncture-proof cardboard boxes with blue marking) — glassware (broken or discarded, contaminated — vials, ampoules, slides) and metallic body implants; treatment — disinfection (sodium hypochlorite) or autoclaving, then recycling. Liquid waste is pre-treated (disinfected) before discharge into drains; general waste goes to black bags (municipal). Microbiology laboratory considerations: cultures and stocks, blood and body fluid samples, discarded specimens, disposable plates — autoclaved in the laboratory before leaving; spill management with 1% hypochlorite; staff PPE and HBV vaccination; needle-stick protocols. Treatment technologies: incineration (double-chamber, 800–1050 °C), plasma pyrolysis, autoclaving (121 °C, 15 psi, 60 min or as validated), microwaving, hydroclaving, shredding, deep burial, chemical disinfection, encapsulation, sharps pits.",
[["Colour coding (BMW Rules 2016)",[["Colour","Waste","Treatment"],["Yellow","Anatomical, soiled dressings, expired drugs, chemical, microbiology/lab waste (pre-treated)","Incineration, plasma pyrolysis, deep burial"],["Red","Contaminated plastics: tubing, catheters, IV sets, gloves, syringes without needles","Autoclave/microwave + shredding → recycling"],["White (translucent)","Sharps: needles, blades, scalpels","Autoclave + shredding / encapsulation"],["Blue","Glassware, metallic implants","Disinfection/autoclave → recycling"]]],
["Key requirements",["Segregation at source","Non-chlorinated bags, bar-coding","Pre-treatment of lab and highly infectious waste","Storage ≤ 48 h","Training, PPE, HBV vaccination of handlers","Records and annual reporting"]]],
{c:"Segregation at source into colour-coded containers, on-site pre-treatment of laboratory waste and safe final disposal protect health workers, the community and the environment."});
